\title{FAQ - How do I refer to an axis?}
\description{
Functions that work along an axis typically allow you to provide it by
position or by title.
}
\section{Positions}{
A position counts axes from \code{1}. Axis \code{1} runs down the rows, axis \code{2}
runs across the columns, and so on.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- array(
  1:24,
  dim = c(2, 3, 4),
  dimnames = list(row = NULL, col = NULL, page = NULL)
)

rray_dimensions(rray_sum(x, axes = 1))
#> [1] 1 3 4
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Titles}{
Titles are the names of the dimension names list, like \code{row} in
\code{list(row = NULL)}. \code{\link[=rray_titles]{rray_titles()}} returns them. A
title works anywhere a position does:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{rray_titles(x)
#> [1] "row"  "col"  "page"

identical(rray_sum(x, axes = "row"), rray_sum(x, axes = 1))
#> [1] TRUE

identical(rray_sum(x, axes = c("page", "row")), rray_sum(x, axes = c(1, 3)))
#> [1] TRUE
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Positions in the result}{
Some arguments specify a position in the result rather than an axis of
\code{x}, like \code{axes} in \code{\link[=rray_insert_axes]{rray_insert_axes()}} and
\code{.axis} in \code{\link[=rray_stack]{rray_stack()}}. That axis doesn’t exist yet,
so it has no title, and these arguments only take positions:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{rray_dimensions(rray_insert_axes(x, axes = 1))
#> [1] 1 2 3 4

rray_insert_axes(x, axes = "row")
#> Error in `rray_insert_axes()`:
#> ! `axes` must be an integer or double vector, not the string "row".
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{\link[=rray_move_axes]{rray_move_axes()}} only takes positions too. \code{to} is
a position in the result, and \code{from} pairs with it.
}

